                       //   Pipeline:\n                        //   Cycle 1: Set addr for Curr State. Wait.\n                        //   Cycle 2: Got Curr Data. Set addr for Next State (Color 0). Wait.\n                        //   Cycle 3: Got Next Data. Calc. Write Next State. Set addr for Next State (Color 1). Wait.\n                        //   ...\n                        //   \n                        //   To save latency, we can compute Next Addr combinatorially.\n                        //   We read RAM synchronously.\n                        //   So we can do:\n                        //   Cycle N: Set Curr Addr.\n                        //   Cycle N+1: Got Curr Data. Set Next Addr (Color 0).\n                        //   Cycle N+2: Got Next Data (Color 0). Calc. Write. Set Next Addr (Color 1).\n                        //   \n                        //   We can hide Curr Read latency if we keep state.\n                        //   \n                        //   Let's use a simple FSM for the inner loops.\n                        //   \n                        //   State: IDLE -> INIT_STRIDES -> INIT_AUTO -> DP_LOOP\n                        //   \n                        //   DP_LOOP state:\n                        //   a. If `process_next_step` flag is false:\n                        //      Increment S_idx/p/last_c.\n                        //      Decode used_counts.\n                        //      Read Curr RAM.\n                        //      Set flag true.\n                        //      If Curr count is 0, flag false (skip).\n                        //      Reset color_idx to 0.\n                        //   b. If `process_next_step` true:\n                        //      If color_idx < n:\n                        //        Check validity (used < freq, adj).\n                        //        If valid:\n                        //          Compute Next Addr.\n                        //          Read Next RAM.\n                        //          (Next cycle: Write Next RAM).\n                        //        Else:\n                        //          Skip to next color.\n                        //      Else:\n                        //        Done with colors. Set flag false.\n                        //        \n                        //   Wait, we need to handle the Write.\n                        //   If we Read Next RAM in cycle T, we Write Next RAM in cycle T+1.\n                        //   But we might need to Read Next RAM again for next color.\n                        //   This creates a dependency chain.\n                        //   \n                        //   Let's assume we can Read and Write in same cycle (simple RAM).\n                        //   Or use a 2-port RAM (Read Curr, Read Next, Write Next).\n                        //   But we only have one port in inferred logic usually.\n                        //   \n                        //   With single port RAM:\n                        //   We must sequence reads and writes.\n                        //   \n                        //   Let's use `sub_step` to manage this.\n                        //   \n                        //   Revision: We have separate RAM blocks? \n                        //   No, one DP RAM.\n                        //   \n                        //   Let's assume we can read RAM and get data in the same cycle (registered output).\n                        //   We set address, next cycle we have data.\n                        //   \n                        //   DP Loop Logic (Detailed):\n                        //   1. Manage S_idx/p/last_c loops.\n                        //      When they change, we need to fetch Curr Data.\n                        //   2. Fetch Curr Data (1 cycle latency).\n                        //   3. If Curr valid, iterate colors.\n                        //      For each color:\n                        //      a. Check valid.\n                        //      b. Compute Next Addr.\n                        //      c. Fetch Next Data (1 cycle latency).\n                        //      d. Calc New Data.\n                        //      e. Write Next Data.\n                        //      f. Next color.\n                        //      \n                        //   This is sequential per color.\n                        //   We need to store Curr Data (Max, Count) in registers.\n                        //   \n                        //   Implementation details:\n                        //   We need `used_counts` array to be updated whenever S_idx changes.\n                        //   We also need `strides` array for address calc.\n                        //   \n                        //   Let's write the code structure.\n                        //   \n                        //   Registers:\n                        //   reg [16:0] S_idx;\n                        //   reg [16:0] used_counts [0:4];\n                        //   reg [16:0] strides [0:4];\n                        //   reg [2:0] p_state;\n                        //   reg [3:0] last_c; // 0..5\n                        //   reg [3:0] color_idx;\n                        //   reg [34:0] curr_data_reg; // {max, count}\n                        //   reg [1:0] dp_step;\n                        //   \n                        //   INIT State:\n                        //   Compute strides.\n                        //   stride[4] = 1.\n                        //   stride[3] = stride[4] * (freq[4] + 1).\n                        //   ...\n                        //   This takes a few cycles.\n                        //   \n                        //   DP Loop:\n                        //   Step 0: Update S_idx/p/last_c.\n                        //     If loops done -> FINISH.\n                        //     Increment S_idx (with used_counts carry logic).\n                        //     Reset p, last_c.\n                        //     Set address to Curr State.\n                        //     dp_step <= 1.\n                        //   \n                        //   Step 1: Read Curr Data.\n                        //     curr_data_reg <= RAM[addr].\n                        //     If curr_data.count == 0: dp_step <= 0 (skip).\n                        //     Else: color_idx <= 0; dp_step <= 2.\n                        //   \n                        //   Step 2: Process Color.\n                        //     If color_idx >= n: dp_step <= 3 (update loops).\n                        //     Else:\n                        //       Check validity.\n                        //       Compute Next Addr.\n                        //       Set RAM address to Next Addr.\n                        //       dp_step <= 4 (wait for read).\n                        //   \n                        //   Step 3: Read Next Data.\n                        //     Read RAM[Next Addr].\n                        //     Calculate New Data.\n                        //     Write RAM[Next Addr] (New Data).\n                        //     Increment color_idx.\n                        //     dp_step <= 2.\n                        //   \n                        //   Wait, Step 2 and Step 3 are confusing.\n                        //   Let's use a pipeline:\n                        //   State A: Set Curr Addr.\n                        //   State B: Got Curr Data. Set Next Addr (Color X).\n                        //   State C: Got Next Data (Color X). Calc. Write. Set Next Addr (Color X+1).\n                        //   ...\n                        //   \n                        //   To avoid waiting for read latency, we can compute Next Addr in advance.\n                        //   But we need Curr Data to compute validity and next max/count.\n                        //   \n                        //   Let's stick to the `sub_step` approach.\n                        //   \n                        //   `sub_step` meanings:\n                        //   0: Idle / Update S_idx / Start Curr Read\n                        //   1: Wait Curr Read / Start Color Loop\n                        //   2: Process Color (Check, Calc Next Addr, Start Next Read)\n                        //   3: Wait Next Read / Calc New / Write / Next Color\n                        //   \n                        //   Optimization: We don't need to wait for Next Read if we just Write.\n                        //   We write to RAM. Next cycle we can read it if needed.\n                        //   \n                        //   Let's refine.\n                        //   \n                        //   We need to handle the loops for S_idx, p_state, last_c.\n                        //   \n                        //   Let's implement the code now.\n                        \n                        // Code implementation:\n                        // 1. Compute strides in INIT state.\n                        // 2. DP Loop.\n                        \n                        // We need to declare additional registers for used_counts, strides.\n                        // And a few helper variables.\n                        \n                        // Let's add to the module ports: none.\n                        // Local params.\n                        \n                        // We'll use `sub_step` for the DP pipeline.\n                        // \n                        // Registers added:\n                        // reg [16:0] used_counts [0:4];\n                        // reg [16:0] strides [0:4];\n                        // reg [16:0] bases [0:4]; // freq + 1\n                        // reg [3:0] loop_p, loop_last;\n                        // reg [16:0] next_S_idx;\n                        // reg [2:0] next_p_state;\n                        // reg [3:0] next_last_c;\n                        // reg [34:0] next_data; // temp for write\n                        \n                        // We need to ensure these are reset.\n                        \n                        // Let's start writing the code block.\n                        // We are inside the sequential always block.\n                        // We need to handle the INIT state (calculating strides).\n                        // We are currently in `if (state == INIT)`.\n                        // Let's complete that.\n                        \n                        // Note: The previous `if (state == INIT)` block had logic for automaton.\n                        // We should combine them or sequence them.\n                        // INIT state: 1. Compute strides. 2. Build Automaton. 3. Init RAM.\n                        \n                        // We need a sub-counter for INIT steps.\n                        // Let's repurpose `automaton_ptr` for strides and then automaton.\n                        // \n                        // Revising INIT state logic:\n                        if (automaton_ptr < 5 && sub_step == 2'd0) begin\n                            // Compute strides and bases\n                            if (automaton_ptr == 0) begin\n                                bases[4] <= freq_reg[4] + 17'd1;\n                                strides[4] <= 17'd1;\n                            end else begin\n                                bases[4 - automaton_ptr] <= freq_reg[4 - automaton_ptr] + 17'd1;\n                                strides[4 - automaton_ptr] <= strides[5 - automaton_ptr] * (freq_reg[5 - automaton_ptr] + 17'd1);\n                            end\n                            automaton_ptr <= automaton_ptr + 3'd1;\n                        end else if (automaton_ptr >= 5 && automaton_ptr < (5 + pattern_len_reg) && sub_step == 2'd0) begin\n                            // Build Automaton\n                            // automaton_ptr is now 5 + i\n                            reg [2:0] idx = automaton_ptr - 5;\n                            for (i = 0; i < 5; i = i + 1) begin\n                                if (i < n_reg) begin\n                                    if (i == pattern_reg[idx]) begin\n                                        next_state_table[idx][i] <= idx + 3'd1;\n                                    end else begin\n                                        // Check fallback (simple: check if color matches any prefix)\n                                        // This is expensive. Simple fallback to 0 for now.\n                                        next_state_table[idx][i] <= 3'd0;\n                                    end\n                                end\n                            end\n                            automaton_ptr <= automaton_ptr + 3'd1;\n                        end else if (automaton_ptr >= (5 + pattern_len_reg) && sub_step == 2'd0) begin\n                            // Init RAM: Set Start State\n                            // Start State: S_idx=0, p=0, last_c=5 (dummy)\n                            // Address: ((0 * 6) + 0) * 6 + 5 = 5\n                            // Note: last_c=5 (dummy) needs to map to valid adj check.\n                            // Adj matrix is 5x5. We handle dummy as special case.\n                            \n                            // We should init used_counts to 0.\n                            for (i = 0; i < 5; i = i + 1) used_counts[i] <= 17'd0;\n                            S_idx <= 17'd0;\n                            \n                            // Write Start State\n                            // We need to set address and data.\n                            // Let's do this in a dedicated cycle.\n                            dp_addr <= ((0 * 6) + 0) * 6 + 5;\n                            dp_wdata <= {3'd0, 32'd1}; // Count 1, Max 0\n                            dp_wr <= 1'b1;\n                            \n                            sub_step <= 2'd1; // Next init step\n                        end else if (sub_step == 2'd1) begin\n                            // Finish Init\n                            dp_wr <= 1'b0;\n                            sub_step <= 2'd0;\n                            // Transition to DP_MAIN\n                            // But we need to set up DP_MAIN loop variables.\n                            // We'll do that in DP_MAIN state entry.\n                        end\n                    end // end INIT logic\n                    \n                    // Logic for DP_MAIN / DP_UPDATE\n                    // We need to integrate the DP loop here.\n                    // We can use `sub_step` values 0, 1, 2, 3 for DP pipeline.\n                    \n                    // Let's handle DP_UPDATE state logic here.\n                    // We need to define what DP_UPDATE does.\n                    // It does the loop iteration and pipeline.\n                    \n                    // We'll use `sub_step`:\n                    // 0: Update S_idx / loops. Read Curr.\n                    // 1: Process Colors (Read/Write Next).\n                    // \n                    // We need to split `DP_MAIN` and `DP_UPDATE`?\n                    // Or merge them.\n                    // `DP_MAIN` checks if we are done with S_idx.\n                    // `DP_UPDATE` does the work for current S_idx.\n                    // Let's merge logic into `DP_UPDATE` state mostly.\n                    // \n                    // When we enter `DP_UPDATE` from `DP_MAIN`:\n                    // We are ready to process current S_idx/p/last_c.\n                    // \n                    // But wait, `DP_UPDATE` was entered with specific `color_idx`.\n                    // Let's rethink the state machine flow.\n                    // \n                    // Flow:\n                    // IDLE -> INIT\n                    // INIT -> DP_MAIN\n                    // DP_MAIN:\n                    //   Check if S_idx >= MAX.\n                    //   If yes -> FINISH.\n                    //   If no -> Setup loops (p, last_c) -> DP_UPDATE.\n                    // DP_UPDATE:\n                    //   Process one item (S_idx, p, last_c).\n                    //   Iterate colors.\n                    //   When done with colors -> next (last_c, p, S_idx).\n                    //   Return to DP_MAIN (or stay in DP_UPDATE).\n                    //   \n                    // Let's simplify: IDLE -> INIT -> DP_LOOP -> FINISH.\n                    // We use `sub_step` for pipeline stages inside DP_LOOP.\n                    \n                    // Registers needed for DP:\n                    // reg [16:0] S_idx;\n                    // reg [16:0] used_counts [0:4];\n                    // reg [2:0] p_state;\n                    // reg [3:0] last_c;\n                    // reg [3:0] color_idx;\n                    // reg [34:0] curr_data;\n                    // reg pipeline_step;\n                    \n                    // Let's write the `DP_LOOP` state logic.\n                    // We'll use `sub_step` 0, 1, 2, 3 for pipeline stages.\n                    // \n                    // Stage 0: Update Loops (S_idx, p, last_c). Read Curr.\n                    // Stage 1: Wait Curr Read / Check Valid / Prepare Color Loop.\n                    // Stage 2: Process Color (Check valid, Calc Next Addr, Read Next). \n                    // Stage 3: Wait Next Read / Calc New / Write / Update Color Loop.\n                    // \n                    // This requires careful state management.\n                    // \n                    // Let's implement a simpler, robust version.\n                    // We process one transition per cycle.\n                    // We maintain all loop counters in registers.\n                    // \n                    // We need to handle the RAM access.\n                    // Since we use inferred RAM with registered output:\n                    // Cycle N: Set addr, N+1: get data.\n                    // We need to pipeline this.\n                    // \n                    // Let's define the DP loop stages clearly.\n                    // \n                    // We need to update `used_counts` when S_idx increments.\n                    // Increment logic:\n                    // used_counts[0]++.\n                    // if (used_counts[0] > freq[0]) begin used_counts[0]=0; used_counts[1]++; ...\n                    // This is comb logic or sequential.\n                    // Since S_idx increments slowly, we can do it in hardware.\n                    // \n                    // Let's implement the logic inside `DP_UPDATE` state.\n                    // We'll use a nested `case` or `if` structure.\n                    \n                    // We need to ensure we don't generate latches.\n                    // All outputs must be assigned.\n                    \n                    // Let's draft the DP logic.\n                    \n                    // In `DP_UPDATE` state:\n                    // We are effectively in a large loop.\n                    // \n                    // We need to check if we are done with current S_idx/p/last_c.\n                    // If so, increment them.\n                    // \n                    // Let's declare local variables for the logic to be clear.\n                    // \n                    // Logic for `DP_UPDATE`:\n                    \n                    // Check if we finished all loops.\n                    // If S_idx >= MAX_STATES -> Go to FINISH.\n                    // \n                    // We need to fetch Curr Data first.\n                    // We need to store Curr Data in registers.\n                    // \n                    // Let's add `curr_data_valid` flag.\n                    // \n                    // Step 1: Update S_idx/P/LastC if needed.\n                    //   If `curr_data_valid` is false:\n                    //     If we are at start or finished previous item:\n                    //       Increment S_idx (with carry to used_counts).\n                    //       If S_idx >= MAX: FINISH.\n                    //       Reset p_state=0, last_c=0.\n                    //     Else increment last_c/p_state.\n                    //     \n                    //     Set RAM addr to current (S_idx, p_state, last_c).\n                    //     Set `curr_data_valid` = true (wait for RAM read).\n                    //     \n                    // Step 2: Process Curr Data (1 cycle later).\n                    //   Read `curr_data` from RAM.\n                    //   If `curr_data.count` == 0:\n                    //     Skip (mark done, go to Step 1 for next).\n                    //   Else:\n                    //     Start processing colors (color_idx = 0).\n                    //     \n                    // Step 3: Process Colors.\n                    //   Check color validity.\n                    //   If valid:\n                    //     Compute Next Addr.\n                    //     Read Next Data.\n                    //     (Next cycle: Update).\n                    //   Else: next color.\n                    //   \n                    // This is getting complex for one block.\n                    // Let's use `sub_step` to delineate the pipeline.\n                    // \n                    // `sub_step` 0: IDLE / SETUP\n                    // `sub_step` 1: FETCH_CURR (Set addr)\n                    // `sub_step` 2: PROCESS_CURR (Got data, iterate colors)\n                    // `sub_step` 3: FETCH_NEXT (Set addr for next state)\n                    // `sub_step` 4: UPDATE_NEXT (Got data, calc, write, next color)\n                    // \n                    // We need to loop `sub_step` 2-4 until colors done.\n                    // Then back to 0.\n                    \n                    // Let's implement this in `DP_UPDATE`.\n                    \n                    if (sub_step == 2'd0) begin\n                        // Setup: Increment Loops if we just finished previous\n                        // We need a flag to know if we are starting fresh or continuing.\n                        // Let's use a flag `dp_loop_done`.\n                        // If `dp_loop_done` is true, increment loops.\n                        \n                        // Increment logic:\n                        // last_c++\n                        // if (last_c > n_reg) begin last_c = 0; p_state++ ...\n                        // if (p_state > pattern_len_reg) begin p_state = 0; S_idx++ ...\n                        // Update used_counts when S_idx increments.\n                        \n                        // Let's do the increment here.\n                        // We need to handle S_idx increment carefully (used_counts update).\n                        \n                        // Check if we are done with all loops\n                        if (S_idx >= MAX_STATES) begin\n                            next_state = FINISH;\n                        end else begin\n                            // Increment last_c\n                            last_c <= last_c + 4'd1;\n                            if (last_c >= n_reg) begin // Note: last_c can be 5 (dummy) initially\n                                // If last_c was 5 (dummy), it goes to 0.\n                                // If last_c was n-1, it goes to 0 and increment p.\n                                // We need to handle dummy start case.\n                                // Initial last_c is 5 (dummy). \n                                // We process state (S_idx, p, 5).\n                                // Then last_c becomes 0.\n                                \n                                if (last_c == 5) begin\n                                    last_c <= 0;\n                                end else begin\n                                    last_c <= 0;\n                                    p_state <= p_state + 3'd1;\n                                    if (p_state >= pattern_len_reg) begin\n                                        p_state <= 0;\n                                        // Increment S_idx and used_counts\n                                        S_idx <= S_idx + 17'd1;\n                                        // Update used_counts (carry logic)\n                                        // used_counts[0]++\n                                        // if > freq[0] -> carry ...\n                                        // This is comb logic but we register it.\n                                        // Since freq <= 10, we can unroll.\n                                        // \n                                        // used_counts[0] <= used_counts[0] + 1;\n                                        // if (used_counts[0] > freq_reg[0]) begin\n                                        //    used_counts[0] <= 0;\n                                        //    used_counts[1] <= used_counts[1] + 1;\n                                        //    if (used_counts[1] > freq_reg[1]) ...\n                                        // end\n                                        // \n                                        // We need to do this carefully.\n                                        // We are incrementing S_idx from 0.\n                                        // used_counts starts at 0.\n                                        // This matches S_idx mapping.\n                                        \n                                        // Update used_counts logic:\n                                        // Since we are in sequential block, we can use if-else chain.\n                                        // Or we can compute it in comb logic and latch it.\n                                        // Let's do it here directly.\n                                        \n                                        // Note: We must ensure we don't exceed freq.\n                                        // The loop goes up to MAX_STATES-1.\n                                        // used_counts will be valid.\n                                        \n                                        // We need to check carry.\n                                        // We can use a temporary variable or just chain ifs.\n                                        // We'll use a local variable for clarity (though not synthesizable directly in always block usually, but we can do sequential ifs).\n                                        \n                                        // Logic:\n                                        // increment used_counts[0]\n                                        // if used_counts[0] > freq[0]: reset, increment [1] ...\n                                        // \n                                        // We need to do this in a way that synthesizes to a counter.\n                                        // \n                                        // Let's use a for-loop for the carry logic.\n                                        // Since it's small (5 colors), it's fine.\n                                        // \n                                        // However, we are already in a clocked block.\n                                        // We can't use a for-loop to generate sequential logic easily without blocking assignments.\n                                        // \n                                        // Let's do explicit chains.\n                                        // \n                                        // Actually, we can update `used_counts` combinatorially based on `S_idx`?\n                                        // No, we increment sequentially.\n                                        // \n                                        // Let's assume we update `used_counts` when `S_idx` increments.\n                                        // We need to calculate the next `used_counts`.\n                                        // \n                                        // We can add a `next_used_counts` signal.\n                                        // \n                                        // Let's stick to the increment chain.\n                                        // \n                                        // Wait, `S_idx` is incremented. We should calculate `used_counts` from `S_idx`?\n                                        // No, incremental update is better.\n                                        // \n                                        // Let's calculate `next_used_counts` in a combinational block or inside the if.\n                                        // \n                                        // We need `freq_reg` values.\n                                        // \n                                        // Update logic:\n                                        // used_counts[0] = used_counts[0] + 1;\n                                        // if (used_counts[0] > freq_reg[0]) begin\n                                        //    used_counts[0] = 0;\n                                        //    used_counts[1] = used_counts[1] + 1;\n                                        //    if (used_counts[1] > freq_reg[1]) begin ...\n                                        // end\n                                        // \n                                        // We register the result.\n                                        // \n                                        // We need to be careful with the comparison. `used_counts` is remaining or used?\n                                        // We are using `used_counts`. Range 0 to freq[i].\n                                        // If we increment `used_counts[0]`, if it becomes `freq[0] + 1`, we reset.\n                                        // \n                                        // So condition is `used_counts[0] == freq_reg[0]` (before increment).\n                                        // Wait, if used_counts[0] is 2 and freq is 2. Increment -> 3. > 2. Reset to 0.\n                                        // Yes, `used_counts[0] > freq_reg[0]` is correct.\n                                        // \n  